#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175689 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebfe6309-b8a3-5e4c-b2ae-495a91bcc433" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Higher Moments of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "69fdace0-5c5b-519b-b03b-40fd523915f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10128" :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCalculate $\\textrm E[X^3].$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of $X^3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X^3] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^3 \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each cubed value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{3}\\cdot 0.15+{2}^{3}\\cdot 0.25+{3}^{3}\\cdot 0.5+{4}^{3}\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.15+8\\cdot 0.25+27\\cdot 0.5+64\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0.15+2+13.5+6.4 \\\\  & =22.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175804 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33fca834-0063-5252-832f-5efb4be6e599" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Properties of Expected Value"}] :question/id #uuid "5e71fcd7-b782-5399-979f-fd62607b5653" :question/math-academy-id "e-7421" :question/problem "Compute $\\textrm E[X(X-3) + 4]$ if $\\textrm E[X^2] = 4$ and $\\textrm E[X] = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding out the product inside the expected value, using the properties of the expected value, and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X(X-3)+4] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}-3X+4] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-3\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[4] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-3\\text{E}[X]+4 \\\\  & =4-3(1)+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175991 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c105dd8-6739-5889-a529-9182ea814d76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "46566681-c2e4-53e1-83c4-d438cabc8066" :question/math-academy-id "e-9106" :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $0.05$ |\n\nCalculate $\\textrm E[X^2].$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of $X^2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each squared value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.15+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.35+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.15+9\\cdot 0.35+16\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =4.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33fca834-0063-5252-832f-5efb4be6e599" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Properties of Expected Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bc64c55-cc10-5bf4-80a0-019a0f33bb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416510} {:answer/id #uuid "4209d557-5cad-5013-a9c1-65ea6323cb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416513} {:answer/id #uuid "d9824405-03bd-5272-8234-ee03e3aa2f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/re